WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.694
Lesson one's camera had no lens, just a hole a quarter of a millimetre wide. It worked.

2
00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.812
But in shade you counted three seconds, and by a window twenty.

3
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:15.625
This front plate adds one small piece of plastic: a lens, twelve millimetres across.

4
00:00:16.033 --> 00:00:19.645
If a hole already makes a picture, why does anyone need a lens?

5
00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:24.114
Most people would say a lens makes things bigger. It magnifies.

6
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:30.525
But this camera's picture is always 36 by 24 millimetres. Nothing needs to be bigger.

7
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:36.686
So why not make the hole bigger, as wide as the large dot: 3.34 millimetres?

8
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:43.639
Now every point of the tree lands as a disc 3.34 millimetres wide. The picture is a smear.

9
00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:50.134
In 1568, Daniele Barbaro put a glass made for spectacles in the hole of a dark room.

10
00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:55.542
He moved the paper until the picture was sharp, and a smaller opening made it clearer still.

11
00:00:55.967 --> 00:01:01.804
In 1611, Johannes Kepler worked out why: how a lens bends light into a picture.

12
00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:05.749
Light from one point of the tree spreads out over the whole lens.

13
00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:10.608
The curved glass bends each ray, the outer ones most, so they all meet again.

14
00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:16.415
One point of the tree, one point on the film. Bright, because the whole lens gathered it.

15
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.417
The same for every point. So a lens does not magnify. It gathers.

16
00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:27.122
But only at one distance. Move the film, and every point spreads into a disc again.

17
00:01:27.533 --> 00:01:31.931
A single lens has a flaw: points at the side of the view focus too soon.

18
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:35.880
The sharp picture does not lie flat. It lies on a curve.

19
00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.920
So on flat film the middle is sharp, and the edges are soft.

20
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:47.665
In 1812, William Wollaston bent a single lens into a shallow bowl, a meniscus, with a stop in front.

21
00:01:48.067 --> 00:01:55.514
In 1840, Joseph Petzval worked out a lens by calculation, the first. Bright, but soft at the edges too.

22
00:01:55.933 --> 00:02:02.302
Sharp edges on flat film take more glass. Your camera has one lens, so it bends the film instead.

23
00:02:02.733 --> 00:02:09.753
The film lies on a curve of 32 millimetres radius, centred on the lens. That curve gave the camera its name.

24
00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:14.462
Now the film follows the sharp picture, from the middle out toward the ends.

25
00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:22.427
The lens is set for 3.7 metres. At the large dot, the middle is sharp from 1.5 metres to far away.

26
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:26.597
Toward the ends it is a little softer, like a single-use camera.

27
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:31.076
Now the sum. How much more light does the lens let in than the pinhole?

28
00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:39.382
The pinhole is f/120. The lens, at its large dot, is f/9.6: we call it f/9.5.

29
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:44.011
The light goes with one over the f-number, squared. Divide, then square.

30
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:53.358
120 divided by 9.6 is 12.5. And 12.5 times 12.5 is about 156.

31
00:02:53.767 --> 00:02:57.388
So the lens lets in about 150 times the light.

32
00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.598
A one-second count becomes less than a hundredth of a second: too short to count.

34
00:03:08.167 --> 00:03:13.373
The lens presses into the round pocket on its front, bulge outward, until it stops.

35
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.791
One plastic lens, focal length 32 millimetres, fixed focus. There is nothing to turn.

36
00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:25.491
The first Kodak camera, in 1888, was fixed focus too: point, press, done.

37
00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.657
Set the large dot. The rocker's quick blink is lesson five.

38
00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:36.247
So why a lens? A hole makes a picture by throwing light away. A lens gathers it back to a point.

39
00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:42.421
Look. A glass ball, or a full glass of water, shows the room upside down. It is a lens.

40
00:03:42.833 --> 00:03:48.971
Try. Hold a magnifying glass between a window and white paper. Move it until the window appears.

41
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:53.398
Next, lesson three: how does a strip of plastic remember a picture?

42
00:03:53.800 --> 00:04:00.326
Every picture here is drawn from the model or simulated. No photograph has been taken with this camera yet.
